.TH GIT-WT 1 "2026-07-29" "gitplus" "gitplus Manual"
.SH NAME
git-wt \- check out a branch here, freeing it from another worktree first
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git wt
.RI ( BRANCH | PR# | URL |.|@)
.br
.B git wt
[\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A branch can only ever be checked out in one worktree at a time (a hard git
rule) \(em normally a hard stop:
.B "git pr"
and
.B "git sync"
just refuse and report where the branch already is.
.B git wt
instead frees it: it detaches the
.I other
worktree's HEAD at its current commit (safe \(em doesn't touch that
worktree's working tree or any uncommitted changes there at all, it just
stops it holding the branch name), then checks the branch out in the
.B current
worktree. A purely local operation with no shell integration needed, since
it never has to move you anywhere.
.PP
The argument can be a branch name, PR number, PR URL, or
.RI ' . '/' @ '
(the current branch) \(em same grammar as
.BR "git pr" / "git sync" .
A PR number or URL is resolved to its head branch via
.B gh
first.
.PP
.B "git pr <id>"
uses the same free-it-first logic internally, so checking out a PR whose
branch is checked out elsewhere just works instead of refusing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B git wt spec-first
Free
.I spec-first
from wherever it's checked out and switch to it here.
.TP
.B git wt 132
Same, but naming a PR number instead of its head branch.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-sync (1),
.BR git-pr (1),
.BR git-worktree (1)
